From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 08:26:09 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 054/342] libroot: vfork() takes over the heap locks and
 undefers signals

The "partial" vfork() is fork() without any of its preparation: no heap
locking, no runtime loader reinit, and signals stay deferred in the child
(_kern_fork() starts it so; fork() undefers them). But the child's exec()
needs malloc() and the runtime loader. If another thread of the parent held
a heap lock at the moment of the fork, the child waited for it forever, and
with signals deferred, a pending signal made every one of those waits
return at once: the child spun at full CPU, never reaching its exec().

posix_spawn() takes this path for POSIX_SPAWN_SETSID, as net_server does
for its services: net_server then blocked in posix_spawn() and never
configured the interface it had just got a DHCP lease for, leaving the
network down after some boots on a 4-core ODROID-C2.

vfork() now does what fork() does except calling the atfork hooks (and
reinitializing the passwd backend). A test that posix_spawn()s /bin/true
while 8 threads use malloc() hung on its first spawn every time before;
with the change, 6000 spawns went through.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/libroot/posix/unistd/fork.c | 39 +++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/system/libroot/posix/unistd/fork.c b/src/system/libroot/posix/unistd/fork.c
index 333b65bf64..b79c3dbab1 100644
--- a/src/system/libroot/posix/unistd/fork.c
+++ b/src/system/libroot/posix/unistd/fork.c
@@ -133,8 +133,15 @@ __register_atfork(void (*prepare)(void), void (*parent)(void), void (*child)(voi
 }
 
 
-pid_t
-fork(void)
+/*!	fork(), and vfork() with \a callHooks false: a vfork()ed child may only
+	exec() or _exit(), so it does without the atfork hooks. But exec() needs
+	malloc() and the runtime loader, whose locks another thread may hold at
+	this moment, so the heap is always taken over as for fork(). The child
+	also always gets its signals back: _kern_fork() starts it with them
+	deferred, and a deferred signal would interrupt every wait it does.
+*/
+static pid_t
+fork_internal(bool callHooks)
 {
 	thread_id thread;
 	status_t status;
@@ -148,7 +155,8 @@ fork(void)
 	}
 
 	// call preparation hooks
-	call_fork_hooks(sPrepareHooks);
+	if (callHooks)
+		call_fork_hooks(sPrepareHooks);
 	__heap_before_fork();
 
 	thread = _kern_fork();
@@ -160,13 +168,15 @@ fork(void)
 		mutex_init(&sForkLock, FORK_LOCK_NAME);
 		__gRuntimeLoader->reinit_after_fork();
 		__heap_after_fork_child();
-		__reinit_pwd_backend_after_fork();
-
-		call_fork_hooks(sChildHooks);
+		if (callHooks) {
+			__reinit_pwd_backend_after_fork();
+			call_fork_hooks(sChildHooks);
+		}
 	} else {
 		// we are the parent
 		__heap_after_fork_parent();
-		call_fork_hooks(sParentHooks);
+		if (callHooks)
+			call_fork_hooks(sParentHooks);
 		mutex_unlock(&sForkLock);
 	}
 
@@ -182,14 +192,15 @@ fork(void)
 }
 
 
+pid_t
+fork(void)
+{
+	return fork_internal(true);
+}
+
+
 pid_t
 vfork(void)
 {
-	thread_id thread = _kern_fork();
-	if (thread < 0) {
-		// something went wrong
-		__set_errno(thread);
-		thread = -1;
-	}
-	return thread;
+	return fork_internal(false);
 }
